course: dictionary
lesson: 9129
title: Smuggler
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A smuggler is a person who secretly and illegally takes goods or people into or out of a country. The clue is in the word itself: smugglers hide what they are doing, and they break the rules.
explain: «People pay smugglers to cross the English Channel in small, unsafe boats.» Pay tells you smugglers charge money. Unsafe and illegal tell you the crossing is not allowed and can be dangerous. Test your meaning by swapping it in: "People pay people who secretly and illegally take them across the sea." It still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
explain: Smugglers have a long history. In the 1700s, smugglers on the coasts of Cornwall, Devon and Yorkshire brought brandy, tea and tobacco into Britain to avoid paying tax, and they hid the goods in caves. Stories such as «Moonfleet» use smugglers as characters, but real smuggling today can be very dangerous and cruel.
fact: A smuggler secretly and illegally takes goods or people into or out of a country.
fact: Goods are smuggled to avoid tax, or because the goods are banned.
fact: In the 1700s, smugglers on the coasts of Cornwall, Devon and Yorkshire brought in brandy, tea and tobacco to avoid tax, often hiding them in caves.
fact: «Drug smugglers» and «people smugglers» are criminals, and their work can be very dangerous.
fact: «Smugglers' cove» means a small, hidden bay where smugglers landed their goods.
exercise: «People pay smugglers to cross the English Channel in small, unsafe boats.» What is a smuggler? | A police officer who guards the coast | *A person who secretly and illegally takes goods or people across a border | A person who sells train tickets | A person who sails a large ship on holiday
exercise: In the 1700s, smugglers brought brandy and tea to the coast of Cornwall. Why did they hide these goods? | *To avoid paying tax on them | To show them in a museum | To keep them warm | To give them away as presents
exercise: «Smugglers charge money for the crossings.» Find and copy the word that tells you smugglers want payment. | crossings | dangerous | *charge | France
test: Which of these is the best example of a smuggler? | A boy who walks his dog on the beach | A man who buys bread at the shop for his family | *A woman who hides brandy under her coat to avoid paying tax | A girl who writes a letter to her gran
test: Why might goods be smuggled into a country? | *Because they are banned, or to avoid tax | Because the shop has run out of them | Because they are too heavy to carry | Because they are old and need to be repaired
exam: Jayden says a smuggler is a postman who delivers parcels quickly. Is he right? | Yes, and smugglers always deliver to the right address | Yes, smugglers deliver parcels openly to every house | *No, a smuggler is not a postman, because a smuggler works in secret and breaks the law | No, a smuggler secretly and illegally takes goods or people across a border
